  "text": "OLUTION:\nLet's start by considering the function $ f(x) = \\frac{(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k)}{x} $ over the positive real numbers.\n\nTo find the values of $ k $ for which the function achieves its minimum value at exactly two positive real numbers $ x $, we can analyze the behavior of the function.\n\nFirst, we need to find the critical points of $ f(x) $ by taking the derivative with respect to $ x $ and setting it equal to zero.\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have:\n$ f'(x) = \\frac{(x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k) + (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) + (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - k) + (x - 18)(x - 98)(x - k)}{x^2} $\n\nSetting $ f'(x) = 0 $, we have:\n$ (x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k) + (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98) + (x - 18)(x - 72)(x - k) + (x - 18)(x - 98)(x - k) = 0 $\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n$ 3(x - 18)(x - 72)(x - 98)(x - k) = 0 $\n\nThis equation has four solutions: $ x = 18 $, $ x = 72 $, $ x = 98 $, and $ x = k $.\n\nSince the function $ f(x) $ achieves its minimum value at exactly two positive real numbers $ x $, the solutions $ x = 18 $ and $ x = 72 $ must be the same.\n\nThus, we have two cases to consider:\n\nCase 1: $ k = 18 $\nIn this case, the function $ f(x) $ achieves its minimum value at $ x = 18 $ and $ x = 72 $.\n\nCase 2: $ k = 72 $\nIn this case, the function $ f(x) $ achieves its minimum value at $ x = 18 $ and $ x = 72 $.\n\nFor Case 1, the sum of the three values of $ k $ is $ 18 + 72 = 90 $.\n\nFor Case 2, the sum of the three values of $ k $ is $ 18 + 72 = 90 $.\n\nThus, the sum of the three values of $ k $ for which the function $ f(x) $ achieves its minimum value at exactly two positive real numbers $ x $ is $ \\boxed{90} $.",
